       "title": "Medicine for the healthy",
       "text": "A  The main purpose of medicine has always been to treat illness. But recent medical advances now promise to change the lives of healthy people too. We are entering a new age of medicine, in which drugs and biotechnology designed for the sick may soon make people’s minds and bodies much more powerful.\n\nB  Students have long used caffeine, in the form of coffee, to stay awake and think clearly while they study. However, one in ten students at the University of Cambridge has now admitted taking medication to help them work for longer. The pills most commonly used are Ritalin, a medicine used to treat the brain condition ADHD (attention deficit hyperactivity disorder), which stops people concentrating, and Modafinil, which treats narcolepsy, the ‘sudden sleep’ disorder. Both medicines work by stimulating brain activity in patients, but some healthy students take them to stay alert and focused for longer. Although the short-term side effects can seem small, regular use is likely to be dangerous. One student, for example, took Modafinil for four days to help her revise for an exam. She passed the exam, but then collapsed, lost her sight for a short time and spent the night in hospital.\n\nC  There is another way of boosting brain activity. TDCS (transcranial direct current stimulation) involves sending small electrical currents into the brain. This therapy has been used especially to treat people who have lost their memories after strokes. Research at the University of Oxford suggests that TDCS can also help healthy people do better in maths tests. Surprisingly, the effects seem to last for up to six months. Scientists are now discussing a battery-powered cap (nicknamed ‘the Zap Cap’) that people could buy to stimulate the brain in the same way. It may turn out to be quite cheap, but its effects cannot be guaranteed.\n\nD  Of course, it is not only the healthy brain that could benefit from medical advances. For many years, some sportspeople have illegally improved their all-round physical power by using anabolic steroids, drugs first designed to stop muscles wasting away. However, new research promises far more powerful forms of enhancement. Scientists have become increasingly skilled at reproducing parts of the body that have stopped working. They can now put new retinas into the eyes of blind people, for example, or give robotic limbs, controlled by the brain, to people who have lost arms or legs. So how could healthy people benefit from this technology? Some have suggested that security guards could have eye implants to help them see in the dark, or that soldiers might wear extra legs or ‘smart trousers’ to help them cross difficult ground. In fact, the first wearable robot to go on sale has already been launched, so soon people may be able to run 20 miles to school or work without feeling tired.\n\nE  It seems likely, then, that medical innovation could create a society divided into three groups: the sick, the healthy and the super-human. The question is: do we want to live in a society in which some people compete and succeed by using medication? It is a decision that needs to be made in the coming decades."
